>> title: Ox Tails, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, braise, southern, gravy, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: oxtails
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: oxtails, ox tail, braised oxtails, smothered oxtails, oxtail and gravy, slow cooker oxtails, crockpot oxtails
>> pairs-with: cheese-grits, collard-greens, butter-beans, hoppin-john
>> servings: 6
>> time: 9 hr 40 min
>> slack: forgiving — the gravy stays thin all day until you take the lid off and thicken it on purpose; the pot will not do it for you
>> step.2: season, both sides
>> step.3: brown 12 min in a skillet, in batches
>> step.4: braise on low, 9 hr
>> step.5: skim, then slake the starch in on high

Written for low, nine hours. Braise these until the meat slips off the bone without help — short of that you are handing someone a cut of tail to fight, and the gravy never goes sticky either, because the stickiness comes out of the bone. Two cups of stock where the oven takes five: nothing leaves this pot, and oxtail gives up a great deal of its own.

Season @beef oxtails{4%lb}(1.8 kg; cut in sections) with @kosher salt{1%Tbs}, @black pepper{2%tsp}.

Brown @&(~1)seasoned oxtails{} in @vegetable oil{2%Tbs} in a #skillet{} for ~brown{12%min}, in two batches. Skip this and the gravy is grey — the slow cooker will make the meat tender and cannot make it brown.

Slow cook @&(~1)browned oxtails{} with @yellow onions{2%large}(sliced), @garlic{6%cloves}(smashed), @carrots{2}(cut in chunks), @beef stock{2%cup}(475 mL), @dry red wine{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @bay leaves{2}, @dried thyme{1%tsp} in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{9%hr}, lid on.

Skim @&(~1)braised oxtails{}, then stir in @cornstarch{1%Tbs}(8 g; slaked in 2 Tbs cold water) and hold it on high with the lid off for ~thicken{20%min}. If you can wait a day instead, chill the whole pot overnight: the fat sets hard and lifts off in one sheet, which is a cleaner gravy than any skimming and is a thing the pressure version has no hours to allow.
